\ ==== continuations ====

\ Delimited continuations: reset / shift / resume.
\ Exercises the mark-on-rstack design across several patterns.

\ -- 1: basic resume of "2 *" with arg 5 -> 10
: shifter shift ;
: ex1 reset 99 shifter 2 * ;
ex1 swap drop 5 swap resume . cr

\ -- 2: drop k, no resume -> 99 (the pre-shift value, since shift unwinds
\        out of ex1 without running 2*)
ex1 drop . cr

\ -- 3: multi-shot, two resumes of the same k
ex1 swap drop
dup 3 swap resume . cr
    4 swap resume . cr

\ -- 4: pre-shift value preserved on stack after unwind
: ex2 reset 100 shifter ;
ex2 drop . cr

\ -- 5: multi-step captured slice "1 + 2 *", arg 3 -> (3+1)*2 = 8
: ex3 reset 0 shifter 1 + 2 * ;
ex3 swap drop 3 swap resume . cr

\ -- 6: nested resets, inner shift only escapes inner
: shifter-drop shift drop ;
: inner-body reset 1 shifter-drop 2 + ;
: ex4 reset 100 inner-body 1000 + ;
ex4 .s cr

\ -- 7: reset in a word with its own locals; shift unwinds past the locals scope
: locals-reset | seed | reset seed 7 + shifter ;
5 locals-reset drop . cr

\ -- 8: resume after unwinding past a locals scope
: locals-resume | seed | reset seed shifter 100 + ;
5 locals-resume swap drop 3 swap resume . cr

\ -- 9: shift inside a nested execute whose reset-owner has locals
: locals-exec | producer | reset producer execute ;
[: 9 shift :] locals-exec drop . cr

\ -- 10: shift outside reset is a clean error
shift

clear

\ ==== repl unwind recovery ====

\ shift-with with no resume unwinds to the reset, leaving interp->unwinding
\ set. At the top level that unwind escapes without being cleared, and since
\ no error_flag is set, the REPL recovery block never runs -- so every later
\ line silently no-ops (DISPATCH / run_inner bail while unwinding). Clearing
\ unwinding unconditionally per line breaks the wedge: the line after the
\ trigger must still execute. Without the fix the 42 below never prints.
reset [: 99 . cr :] shift-with
42 . cr

clear
